_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 14
---
order: 14
---
short_label: III.14
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Equal straight lines in a circle are equally distant from the center, and those which are equally distant from the center equal one another.
----
proof:

Let {AB} and {CD} be equal straight lines in a circle {ABDC}.

I say that {AB} and {CD} are equally distant from the center.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="270" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.14 diagram" src="propIII14.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // A theorem and its converse on one figure, so one deck walks both
    // halves over the same construction ("with the same construction" is
    // Euclid's own instruction). The construction is short — the center,
    // two perpendiculars, two radii — and each clause is animated; the
    // rest is highlight beats over the I.47 accounting.
    //
    // Joyce's figure draws the halves AF, FB, CG only as parts of the
    // chords, so they get transparent twins (;0;0;0). The circle is the
    // random-filled ABC; the prose calls it ABDC, and that name goes to a
    // transparent twin so a highlight never promotes the fill over the
    // triangles (#140). EAF and ECG are Joyce's two triangles: converted
    // with no faceColor field, so they carry the opaque near-white default
    // — which is what his gif shows, a pale pair on the green — and they
    // are never named in a slide set. No squares are drawn (none would fit
    // a 270px canvas), so "the square on AF" lights AF. The two right
    // angles get red markers, hidden in the static figure. Resting figure
    // pixel-unchanged.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","D","AB","CD","ABC","ABDC"];
    var CENT  = GIVEN.concat(["E"]);
    var PERP  = CENT.concat(["F","G","EF","EG","EA","EC"]);
    var RTF   = PERP.concat(["angAFE"]);
    var HALF  = RTF.concat(["AF","FB"]);
    var RTG   = HALF.concat(["CG","angCGE"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.14",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;135,235",
            "E;point;free;135,135",
            "ABC;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "B;point;circleSlider;ABC,40,70",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "C;point;circleSlider;ABC,150,180",
            "ECD;polygon;similar;E,C,E,B,A;0;0;0;0",
            "D;point;vertex;ECD,3",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "EA;line;connect;E,A",
            "EC;line;connect;E,C",
            "F;point;midpoint;AB",
            "G;point;midpoint;CD",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F",
            "EG;line;connect;E,G",
            "EAF;polygon;triangle;E,A,F;0;0;0",
            "ECG;polygon;triangle;E,C,G;0;0;0",
            // Transparent twin of the circle under the prose's name.
            "ABDC;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;0;0",
            // Halves of the chords; AB and CD already draw them.
            "AF;line;connect;A,F;0;0;0",
            "FB;line;connect;F,B;0;0;0",
            "CG;line;connect;C,G;0;0;0",
            // The right angles at F and G. One marker per vertex, so no
            // radius override.
            "angAFE;sector;angleMarker;F,A,E;0;0;red;red",
            "angCGE;sector;angleMarker;G,C,E;0;0;red;red"
        ],
        // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, E, B, C. A, B and C
        // are the chords' endpoints and belong to slide 1; E is the center,
        // declared as a free point, and the proof takes it on slide 3 — so
        // it is deferred out of the every-slide auto-union. D (vertex) and
        // F, G (midpoint) are derived and listed per slide.
        deferDraggables: ["E"],
        aliases: { "AE": "EA", "CE": "EC", "FA": "AF", "BF": "FB",
                   "GC": "CG", "FE": "EF", "GE": "EG" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”), split at its seams.
            { text: "“Equal straight lines in a circle are equally distant from the center,”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [] },
            { text: "“and those which are equally distant from the center equal one another.”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.14" }] },
            { text: "Let {AB} and {CD} be equal straight lines in a circle {ABDC}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "I say that {AB} and {CD} are equally distant from the center.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "Take the center {E} of the circle {ABDC}.",
              visible: CENT,
              highlighted: ["ABDC", "E"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "E", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the center of the circle", ref: "III.1" }] },
            { text: "Draw {EF} and {EG} from {E} perpendicular to {AB} and {CD}, and join {AE} and {EC}.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "EF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "F",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "EG", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "G",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "EA", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "EC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "perpendiculars from E to AB and CD", ref: "I.12" },
                  { ref: "I.Post.1" } ] },
            { text: "Then, since a straight line {EF} passing through the center cuts a straight line {AB} not passing through the center at right angles, it also bisects it.",
              visible: RTF,
              highlighted: ["EF","AB","E"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angAFE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "EF through the center at right angles to AB bisects it", ref: "III.3" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {AF} equals {FB}. Therefore {AB} is double {AF}.",
              visible: HALF,
              highlighted: ["AF","FB"] },
            { text: "For the same reason {CD} is also double {CG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["CD","CG"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angCGE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "EG through the center at right angles to CD bisects it", ref: "III.3" }] },
            { text: "But {AB} equals {CD}, therefore {AF} also equals {CG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD","AF","CG"] },
            { text: "Also, since {AE} equals {EC}, the square on {AE} also equals the square on {EC}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["EA","EC"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AE and EC are radii of the circle", ref: "I.Def.15" }] },
            { text: "But the sum of the squares on {AF} and {EF} equals the square on {AE}, for the angle at {F} is right,",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AF","EF","EA","angAFE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right angle at F in the triangle EAF", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "and the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GC} equals the square on {EC}, for the angle at {G} is right.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["EG","CG","EC","angCGE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right angle at G in the triangle ECG", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the squares on {AF} and {FE} equals the sum of the squares on {CG} and {GE}, of which the square on {AF} equals the square on {CG}, for {AF} equals {CG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AF","EF","CG","EG"] },
            { text: "Therefore the remaining square on {FE} equals the square on {EG}. Therefore {EF} equals {EG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["EF","EG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "subtract the equal squares on AF and CG", ref: "C.N.3" }] },
            { text: "But straight lines in a circle are said to be equally distant from the center when the perpendiculars drawn to them from the center are equal. Therefore {AB} and {CD} are equally distant from the center.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD","EF","EG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "equal perpendiculars from the center", ref: "III.Def.4" }] },
            // The converse, on the same construction.
            { text: "Next, let the straight lines {AB} and {CD} be equally distant from the center, that is, let {EF} equal {EG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["EF","EG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "equally distant from the center", ref: "III.Def.4" }] },
            { text: "I say that {AB} also equals {CD}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "For, with the same construction, we can prove, as before, that {AB} is double {AF}, and {CD} double {CG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AB","AF","CD","CG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "EF and EG bisect AB and CD", ref: "III.3" }] },
            { text: "And, since {AE} equals {CE}, the square on {AE} equals the square on {CE}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["EA","EC"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AE and CE are radii of the circle", ref: "I.Def.15" }] },
            { text: "But the sum of the squares on {EF} and {FA} equals the square on {AE}, and the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GC} equals the square on {CE}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["EF","AF","EA","EG","CG","EC"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right angles at F and G", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the squares on {EF} and {FA} equals the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GC}, of which the square on {EF} equals the square on {EG}, for {EF} equals {EG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["EF","AF","EG","CG"] },
            { text: "Therefore the remaining square on {AF} equals the square on {CG}. Therefore {AF} equals {CG}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AF","CG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "subtract the equal squares on EF and EG", ref: "C.N.3" }] },
            { text: "And {AB} is double {AF}, and {CD} double {CG}, therefore {AB} equals {CD}.",
              visible: RTG,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.14") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1":  "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Def.15":  "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15/",
        "I.12":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI12/",
        "I.47":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI47/",
        "C.N.3":     "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/",
        "III.1":     "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII1/",
        "III.3":     "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII3/",
        "III.Def.4": "/elements/books/bookIII/definitions/defIII4/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Take the center {E} of the circle {ABDC}. Draw {EF} and {EG} from {E}  perpendicular to {AB} and {CD}, and join {AE} and {EC}. [!just III.1; I.12]

Then, since a straight line {EF} passing through the center cuts a straight line {AB} not passing through the center at right angles, it also bisects it.  Therefore {AF} equals {FB}. Therefore {AB} is double {AF}. [!just III.3]

For the same reason {CD} is also double {CG}. But {AB} equals {CD}, therefore {AF} also equals {CG}.

Also, since {AE} equals {EC}, the square on {AE} also equals the square on {EC}.  But the sum of the squares on {AF} and {EF} equals the square on {AE}, for the angle at {F} is right, and the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GC} equals the square on {EC}, for the angle at {G} is right.  Therefore the sum of the squares on {AF} and {FE} equals the sum of the squares on {CG} and {GE}, of which the square on {AF} equals the square on {CG}, for {AF} equals {CG}.  Therefore the remaining square on {FE} equals the square on {EG}.  Therefore {EF} equals {EG}. [!just I.47]

But straight lines in a circle are said to be equally distant from the center when the perpendiculars drawn to them from the center are equal.  Therefore {AB} and {CD} are equally distant from the center. [!just III.Def.4]

<hr width="50%">

Next, let the straight lines {AB} and {CD} be equally distant from the center, that is, let {EF} equal {EG}.

I say that {AB} also equals {CD}.

For, with the same construction, we can prove, as before, that {AB} is double {AF}, and {CD} double {CG}.  And, since {AE} equals {CE}, the square on {AE} equals the square on {CE}.  But the sum of the squares on {EF} and {FA} equals the square on {AE}, and the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GC} equals the square on {CE}. [!just I.47]

Therefore the sum of the squares on {EF} and {FA} equals the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GC}, of which the square on {EF} equals the square on {EG}, for {EF} equals {EG}. Therefore the remaining square on {AF} equals the square on {CG}.  Therefore {AF} equals {CG}.  And {AB} is double {AF}, and {CD} double {CG}, therefore {AB} equals {CD}.

Therefore *equal straight lines in a circle are equally distant from the center, and those which are equally distant from the center equal one another.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

Note how Euclid has proved twice in the course of this proof the side-side-right angle congruence theorem.  See the [note](/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI26/#cong) after I.26 about congruence theorems for triangles.

This proposition is used in the next one.
